# L §5.14.1 next(K, d): "the first movement of event kind K whose day is on or after d (first(m in K over d ..) ties
# by m.day): two movements on that first day are a tie, refused (RUN-TIE); a reader that needs one of them writes the
# first itself with a fuller ties by, first(m in K over d ..) ties by (m.day, e)". bonus[x] and bonus[y] fall on one
# day: RUN-TIE (n1; index order never breaks a tie, L §5.11), as g1 and g2 of two moves (n2). n3 states the fuller
# ties by (m.day, m.rank), rank = pos(j): bonus[x] (rank 1). (Writer 8: n1 was bonus[x] by key order, removed.)
=== m.tally
model n
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
index jobs = [x, y]

kind wages: event certain
  leg cash: PLN
  attr rank: int
kind gifts: event certain
  leg cash: PLN

input bday: date
input gday: date
input amt: money PLN

place konto: accounts PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place world: outside lives 1900-01-01..

move bonus[j in jobs]: wages
  on bday
  attr rank = pos(j)
  leg cash from world to konto amount amt

move g1: gifts
  on gday
  leg cash from world to konto amount amt

move g2: gifts
  on gday
  leg cash from world to konto amount amt

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-01
  bday = 2026-03-10
  gday = 2026-04-01
  amt = 10.00 PLN

ask n1: next(wages, 2026-03-01)
ask n2: next(gifts, 2026-03-01)
ask n3: first(m in wages over 2026-03-01 ..) ties by (m.day, m.rank)
=== expect
n1: refused: RUN-TIE next(wages, 2026-03-01) has a tie at bonus[x], bonus[y] that changes the result, and its ties by doesn't break it
  cut 2026-01-01
n2: refused: RUN-TIE ...
  cut 2026-01-01
n3: bonus[x]
  cut 2026-01-01
